--- a/docs/userguide/calling.rst
+++ b/docs/userguide/calling.rst
@@ -59,6 +59,9 @@ The API defines a standard set of execution options, as well as three methods:
     - ``T.apply_async(task_id=f'my_own_task_id')``
         sets the id of the task to my_own_task_id instead of a uuid that is normally generated
 
+    - ``T.apply_async(time_limit=10, soft_time_limit=8)``
+        sets the hard and soft time limits for this task invocation.
+
 Example
 -------
 
diff --git a/docs/userguide/tasks.rst b/docs/userguide/tasks.rst
index 0a29e0982d94eefb7bdb0a526fda180e5cb3e927..c2a889dd94650035e43f6677220ebf96c2869c45 100644
--- a/docs/userguide/tasks.rst
+++ b/docs/userguide/tasks.rst
@@ -351,6 +351,24 @@ The request defines the following attributes:
 :timelimit: A tuple of the current ``(soft, hard)`` time limits active for
             this task (if any).
 
+:time_limit: The hard time limit, in seconds, active for this task, or
+             :const:`None` if no hard time limit is configured. This is the
+             first element of :attr:`~@Task.request.timelimit` unpacked into a
+             dedicated attribute.
+
+:soft_time_limit: The soft time limit, in seconds, active for this task, or
+                  :const:`None` if no soft time limit is configured. This is
+                  the second element of :attr:`~@Task.request.timelimit`
+                  unpacked into a dedicated attribute.
+
+.. versionadded:: 5.7
+    The :attr:`~@Task.request.time_limit` and
+    :attr:`~@Task.request.soft_time_limit` request attributes. They are
+    :const:`None` when no limits are configured, reflect the values passed to
+    :meth:`~@Task.apply_async` (``time_limit`` / ``soft_time_limit``), and
+    reflect limits declared on the task class or via the
+    :setting:`task_time_limit` / :setting:`task_soft_time_limit` settings.
+
 :callbacks: A list of signatures to be called if this task returns successfully.
 
 :errbacks: A list of signatures to be called if this task fails.
